WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.120
So yesterday was day two of Donald Trump's second&nbsp;
impeachment trial, Monday, day one was a full&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:12.000
day of debate as to whether the Senate actually&nbsp;
has the jurisdiction to try a former president.&nbsp;

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:19.760
They decided, yes, we do, by a 56 to 44 votes. So&nbsp;
yesterday was sort of like the first real day with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:23.920
eight hours going to the prosecution. We&nbsp;
saw all sorts of different presentations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:28.560
including presentations with&nbsp;
videos from the January 6th riots,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:33.680
some of which will review later video that&nbsp;
was previously not seen, video that showed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:38.160
just how close of a call it was for a lot of&nbsp;
members of the House and Senate avoiding these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:44.880
riotous violent mobs incited by Donald Trump. And&nbsp;
you would think that even though most Republicans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:49.120
have already prejudged this impeachment&nbsp;
trial, most Republicans have already decided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:54.560
they're going to be voting to acquit regardless&nbsp;
of what's going on. You might have thought that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:59.120
they'd have at least pretended to be paying&nbsp;
attention, pretended to be weighing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:04.640
presentations and making some kind of a decision&nbsp;
based on the evidence that was there. But they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:10.400
had the benefit that the cameras did not show&nbsp;
the chamber at any time during the proceedings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:14.240
The cameras, as we saw during yesterday's&nbsp;
live streams and much of the media coverage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:21.280
were focused in quite tightly on the presenter,&nbsp;
the various House impeachment managers who had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:27.840
turns to speak yesterday. And we were left to rely&nbsp;
on reports of members of the media in the chamber&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:33.120
to tell us what exactly were senators doing&nbsp;
during what are very long days of presentations.&nbsp;

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:38.240
And it turns out that many Republicans&nbsp;
were engaged in everything from drawing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:47.600
doodles to sneaking in iPads, as Chuck Grassley&nbsp;
reportedly did, in order to be able to read&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:52.880
other material. Unclear exactly what it&nbsp;
was while the presentations were going on.&nbsp;

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:57.520
A Business Insider has a nice sort of&nbsp;
compilation of different reports about this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:01.840
and they say that many senators were distracting&nbsp;
themselves yesterday afternoon when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:06.240
Democratic impeachment managers&nbsp;
were laying out the case. Rand Paul,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:13.760
who at many points appeared to be the only person&nbsp;
in the chamber not wearing a mask. He was doodling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:18.720
and according to one report, drew a picture&nbsp;
of what appeared to be the Capitol, I guess&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:25.040
almost like architectural drawings of some kind,&nbsp;
according to reporters in the room during a long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:31.360
stretch of the presentation. Rand Paul wasn't&nbsp;
even in the room, but at one point when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:38.560
proceedings went to a break, Rand Paul reportedly&nbsp;
ran back in and gave a note to Trump's attorney,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:45.120
David Schoen. That's according to an ABC News&nbsp;
reporter named Alison Pekkanen. A Cynthia Lummis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:52.160
who's a Wyoming Republican at certain points, was&nbsp;
not wearing a mask as well. Manu Raju reporting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:56.400
that Rand Paul wasn't even at his desk for&nbsp;
much of the 1:00 p.m. hour. That was a key&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:02.880
hour of presentations from the House impeachment&nbsp;
managers. NBC News was able to report that Senator&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:09.440
Mike Braun was sleeping during certain parts of&nbsp;
the presentation. This is not like they slept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:15.440
through metaphorically. Mike Braun was literally&nbsp;
asleep during part of these presentations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:22.320
Rick Scott at one point was looking at a map&nbsp;
of what appeared to be Southeast Asia. Unclear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:27.360
if he was catching up on the geopolitical&nbsp;
implications of the borders in Asia or maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:32.320
figuring out where he wanted to go on an upcoming&nbsp;
trip. A Senator, Josh Hawley, who was one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:37.360
main players in voting against the certification&nbsp;
of a number of different votes on January 6th,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:43.200
at one point was leaning back with his feet up on&nbsp;
the back of a seat in front of him. Very remember&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:48.160
when Barack Obama put his feet up on the desk&nbsp;
in the Oval Office and it was a big scandal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:51.760
almost as big a scandal as when he wore&nbsp;
a tan suit or liked the wrong mustard.&nbsp;

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:55.680
And Republicans said, you don't put your&nbsp;
feet up in the in the Oval Office. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.680 --> 00:04:00.640
Senator Josh Hawley with his feet up on&nbsp;
the seat in front of him in the Senate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:07.600
and he appeared to be reading documents&nbsp;
unrelated to the impeachment trial.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:13.840
And he said he was reading trial&nbsp;
briefs. So this is I mean, listen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:19.440
if this were, you know, a lot of a lot of&nbsp;
Republicans have been making the case that there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:26.880
too much about this trial that's not happening the&nbsp;
way a real trial would happen in a court of law.&nbsp;

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:31.840
For example, many Republicans have taken issue&nbsp;
with the fact that Democratic Senator Patrick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:39.520
Leahy was is not only presiding over the&nbsp;
impeachment trial, but will also be a juror&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:44.960
in that he is one of the hundred votes on the&nbsp;
conviction or acquittal of Donald Trump. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:51.440
Senator Patrick Leahy also is, in a sense, a&nbsp;
witness because he was there on January six. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:56.240
the witness thing would apply to every single one&nbsp;
of these senators since they were all there on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.240 --> 00:05:02.240
January six. So that seems to be neither here nor&nbsp;
there, but. One of the other important differences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:07.680
between a, quote, real trial in a court of&nbsp;
law and what's going on here is that if normal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:16.400
jurors were reading on iPads, sleeping, reading&nbsp;
documents related to other matters during a trial,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:20.960
they would be removed from a case and it could&nbsp;
even potentially lead to a mistrial, depending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:29.120
on what the circumstances were in a normal jury.
Many of the individuals would be disqualified, not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:33.920
potentially because they were so-called&nbsp;
witnesses, but because they were part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:39.680
of the alleged crime that took place.
What I mean by that is that many of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:46.740
senators that will be jurors, Ted Cruz, Josh&nbsp;
Holly and others were actually part of the two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.740 --> 00:05:52.880
month campaign of claiming that the election was&nbsp;
stolen by Joe Biden from Donald Trump. That led&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:59.200
to the January 6th riots in the first place. They&nbsp;
were essentially part of the conspiracy with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:05.840
defendant in this case, Donald Trump. So there's&nbsp;
a lot to sort through here, but I would hope&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:11.680
that everybody would be able to see that sent&nbsp;
reports of senators sleeping and senators sneaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:15.920
in iPads and say, listen, that's not right. Even&nbsp;
if they ultimately are going to vote to acquit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:21.200
they should be paying attention. But&nbsp;
unfortunately, there are lots of right wingers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:26.400
defending this, saying it's a kangaroo court,&nbsp;
it's a sham, it's ridiculous. It's meaningless.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:30.640
It shouldn't even be happening. So good for&nbsp;
these senators for literally sleeping through it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:35.920
It's stunning that this is what's going on.&nbsp;
It's stunning that these are the hundred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.640 --> 00:06:41.280
one hundred of the most powerful people in the&nbsp;
United States. And some of them are napping,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:47.360
literally napping during the proceedings. We'll&nbsp;
see today how it continues. I don't expect Rand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:51.760
Paul to change his behavior. I don't expect Chuck&nbsp;
Grassley to change his behavior. And we'll get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:55.440
into some of the meat of what was presented&nbsp;
yesterday, which was truly stunning to see.

